<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakar of Johor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Abu Bakar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> was temenggung of Johor from 1862, maharaja from 1868 and sultan from 1886. He moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-bahru">Johor Bahru</a>, encouraged Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and gambier planters, and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, cultivating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and the British. In 1895 he gave Johor a written constitution. His diplomacy kept Johor outside the Federated Malay States, and he is remembered as the father of modern Johor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakar-of-johor.jpg" alt="Abu Bakar of Johor" width="250" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Mr Tan at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johor-AbuBakar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Bakar was born on 3 February 1833 in the Istana Temenggong at Teluk Belanga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temenggong-daeng-ibrahim">Temenggong Daeng Ibrahim</a> and a patrilineal descendant of Sultan Abdul Jalil Shah IV. After lessons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">Adat</a>, traditional Malay law, he attended the mission school of Reverend Benjamin Peach Keasberry and learnt fluent English. From 1851 he helped his father in negotiations against Sultan Ali Iskandar, who claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, and took over more of the administration as his father aged. During the Pahang Civil War he backed Tun Mutahir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He became temenggong within three days of his father's death on 31 January 1862 and soon sent a small force to support Tun Koris in Pahang. He expanded the Kangchu system, issuing river documents to the Kapitan Cina who ran gambier and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> plantations along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor-river">Johor River</a>, and employed a Malay administrator who spoke Teochew. Addressed as maharaja during his visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1866, he had his claim examined by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ali-haji">Raja Ali Haji</a>, won the approval of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Lingga and the Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a>, and was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> on 30 June 1868.</p><p>In 1885 he set up a postal service and courts on British lines and the Johor Military Forces. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he asked for recognition as sultan; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, by then a personal friend, consented, and a treaty signed on 11 December 1885 formalised relations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Johor. He travelled widely, meeting Sultan Abdülaziz in 1866, receiving King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> in 1881 and, on tours in 1891 and 1893, Emperor Francis Joseph, King Umberto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Already suffering from Bright's disease, he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in May 1895 to seek support for his rule, caught pneumonia at Bailey's Hotel and died there on 4 June 1895. He was buried at the royal mausoleum of Makam Mahmoodiah on 7 September, and his son Tunku Ibrahim was installed as sultan in November. Neighbouring rulers sought his advice: he persuaded Sultan Ahmad of Pahang to accept a British consultant instead of a Resident, and a British attempt to place a Resident in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> in 1885 failed, so the state kept control of its internal affairs until 1914.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Bakar of Johor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Johor's constitution of 1895, issued under <b>Sultan Abu Bakar</b>, forbade any ruler from surrendering the state or any part of it to a foreign power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Johor, long independent under modernising rulers such as <b>Abu Bakar</b>, accepted a general adviser in 1914.</blockquote><a href="/state/unfederated-malay-states">Unfederated Malay States</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Telok Blangah, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=1.28,103.81&amp;year=1873" aria-label="Telok Blangah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abu Bakar of Johor"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6170 4891h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6170 4891h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=1.28,103.81&amp;year=1873">What was here in 1873? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
